**user**:
> I have started a plan to build an abstraction around model and tool selection in my AI system. Here is the basic structure:
> 
> ===
> 
> # Planner: JavaScript-Orchestrated Execution Notes
> 
> ## Purpose
> 
> `Planner` is a synchronous, chat-first abstraction for selecting and executing AI responses across models and tools.
> 
> For each request, it selects one small, versioned **PlanSpec** before a model examines the task in depth. The selected responder then lets the model produce:
> 
> 1. a useful visible answer; and, optionally,
> 2. a bounded JavaScript execution plan that calls registered tools and models adaptively.
> 
> The system optimizes trustworthy user utility: usefulness, quality, grounding, reliability, cost, latency, and calibrated exploration.
> 
> A **plan** remains the right term: it is the top-level execution mode selected by policy. JavaScript is the adaptive, per-request procedure produced within that mode.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Public API
> 
> ```python
> planner = Planner(conversation_id="...")
> 
> response = planner.chat_response(messages, max_tokens=None)
> stream = planner.stream_response(messages, max_tokens=None)
> 
> planner.vote_up(response.id)
> planner.vote_down(response.id)
> planner.feedback(response.id, kind="correction", value="...")
> 
> planner.poll()          # initially []
> planner.ongoing()       # initially []
> planner.async_status()  # initially {"count": 0, "operations": []}
> 
> planner.explain(response.id)
> ```
> 
> The public interface is blocking and synchronous. Future asynchronous work is internal and exposed through polling, never requiring application code to use `await`.
> 
> Models, tools, and plans are registered in a shared version-aware registry.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Top-level plans
> 
> Keep the candidate set intentionally small. The policy selects among:
> 
> | Plan | Responder | Intended behavior |
> |---|---|---|
> | `javascript_planning` | `JavaScriptPlanningResponder` | Model answers directly and optionally appends JavaScript for further work. |
> | `web_rag` | `WebRagJavaScriptResponder` | Retrieve web evidence first; model answers using that evidence and may append adaptive JavaScript. |
> | `parallel_direct_web` | `ParallelDirectWebJavaScriptResponder` | Generate a private draft and retrieve web evidence concurrently; model produces the visible answer using both and may append JavaScript. |
> 
> These are not rigid task strategies. They are only initial conditions: whether to start with no research, web grounding, or parallel draft-plus-research. The LLM decides subsequent work dynamically through JavaScript.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Request lifecycle
> 
> ```text
> canonical messages
>   → assemble PlannerContext
>   → generate eligible registered PlanSpecs
>   → select a PlanSpec
>   → resolve the concrete model
>   → allocate and persist ResponseRecord
>   → execute selected responder
>   → optionally execute bounded JavaScript
>   → persist runs, traces, artifacts, metrics, and failures
>   → return visible response
> ```
> 
> ### Response and streaming rules
> 
> - Allocate the response ID and persist `ResponseRecord` before provider execution or visible output.
> - A streamed response marks `visible_output_started=True` after its first non-empty visible chunk.
> - A terminal ```plan footer is withheld from visible streaming output.
> - If execution fails before visible output, a future policy may retry another plan.
> - Never silently switch plans/models after visible output begins.
> - Persist final metrics on completion, failure, cancellation, or stream exhaustion.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## JavaScript orchestration
> 
> The model may append one terminal block:
> 
> ```javascript
> ```plan
> async function main(input) {
>   const results = await parallelMap(
>     items,
>     { concurrency: 4 },
>     async item => await tools["search.web"]({ query: item })
>   );
>   return { results };
> }
> ```
> ```
> 
> JavaScript is an orchestration language, not an unrestricted sandbox.
> 
> It may use:
> 
> - explicitly exposed, version-pinned registered tools;
> - bounded `parallelMap`;
> - bounded retry;
> - JSON-safe input and output.
> 
> It may not use:
> 
> - imports, filesystem, network APIs, timers, process globals;
> - `eval`, `Function`, WebAssembly;
> - unbounded loops or unrestricted host access.
> 
> The runtime validates source, enforces operation/parallelism/wall-time/output budgets, runs the sandboxed worker, and mediates every tool call.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Durable execution model
> 
> The system does not need a hand-authored workflow DAG. However, every concrete JavaScript execution remains auditable as a graph-like record of calls and outputs.
> 
> Persist:
> 
> - `ResponseRecord`: selected plan/version, resolved outer model, lifecycle, metrics, feedback attribution;
> - `ExecutionRun`: one JavaScript execution, source, limits, result, status, and parent relationship;
> - `ToolCallTrace`: each JavaScript-mediated tool call, arguments, timing, status, and summarized result;
> - `Artifact`: typed durable outputs;
> - `SourceExcerpt`: exact relevant source passages;
> - `StatePatch`: proposed or approved versioned changes to conversation/project/user state;
> - feedback and evaluation events.
> 
> Canonical user/assistant transcript messages remain append-only. Tool outputs, artifacts, memories, and async results are injected through explicit context assembly, never by rewriting history.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Context and provenance
> 
> `PlannerContext` represents the stable per-request view used by policy and responders:
> 
> - canonical messages;
> - conversation, project, and user identifiers;
> - inferred task/risk classes;
> - estimated token budget;
> - decision features;
> - relevant durable state;
> - eligible artifacts and memories.
> 
> External and retrieved content is always data, not instruction. Context assembly must preserve provenance, trust level, relevance, scope, recency, and prompt-injection risk.
> 
> The current implementation may use transitional message construction, but `ContextAssembler` should eventually own effective-message assembly.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Selection, evaluation, and learning
> 
> The selector chooses only among eligible top-level plans. Its conceptual utility is:
> 
> \[
> U(p \mid x) =
> w_s S +
> w_q Q -
> w_c C -
> w_l L -
> w_r R +
> w_e E
> \]
> 
> Where satisfaction, quality, cost, latency, reliability risk, and exploration remain separate evidence streams.
> 
> Learning sources include:
> 
> - thumbs and richer user feedback;
> - observed provider cost, token usage, latency, and failure;
> - tool failures and execution-run outcomes;
> - task-specific tests and offline evaluation;
> - grounding, citation, calibration, and factuality checks.
> 
> Do not optimize solely for thumbs. Cheap, fluent, weakly grounded answers must not dominate reliable plans through feedback volume alone.
> 
> Plan identity must change when material behavior changes: responder behavior, prompts, tool bindings, configuration, or versioned dependencies.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Core package structure
> 
> ```text
> planner/
>   planner2.py          # synchronous Planner facade
>   types.py             # domain dataclasses and protocols
>   registry.py          # versioned model/tool/plan registry
>   default_plans.py     # three registered top-level plans
>   responders.py        # three JavaScript-capable responders and shared bases
>   selection.py         # plan-selection policy
>   decision.py          # lightweight request cues and contextual evidence
>   model_router.py      # concrete model selection per invocation
>   context.py           # context assembly contract and future implementation
>   metrics.py           # sole lifecycle-metrics mutation interface
>   persistence.py       # repository protocol and in-memory implementation
>   feedback.py          # feedback service contract
>   evaluation.py        # offline/online quality and reliability evidence
>   visualization.py     # explain/debug plan and execution rendering
>   async_api.py         # inert-compatible polling boundary
> 
>   javascript/
>     api.py             # JS tool manifest and planning prompt
>     validate.py        # static safety validation
>     runtime.py         # sandbox worker, budgets, tool mediation
>     worker.js          # constrained JavaScript host
> 
>   tools/
>     ...                # typed ToolInvocation → ToolResult implementations
> ```
> 
> Avoid reintroducing a separate workflow/DAG subsystem unless a real future requirement cannot be represented as persisted/resumable JavaScript execution.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Near-term priorities
> 
> 1. Complete provenance-aware context assembly from state, artifacts, and source excerpts.
> 2. Record metrics for nested JavaScript tool/model calls, including cost and latency.
> 3. Persist and link source excerpts actually used in final answers.
> 4. Apply `StatePatch` objects through explicit policy and reducer logic.
> 5. Implement offline evaluation and quality/reliability evidence.
> 6. Add narrowly scoped pre-visible-output retry behavior.
> 7. Add resumable/deferred JavaScript runs and delivery-policy-controlled async updates only when product needs justify them.
> 
> The central design principle is: **select a small, learnable execution mode; let the model adaptively orchestrate bounded capabilities; persist enough evidence to make the system safe, inspectable, and steadily better.**
> 
