**assistant**:
> Your environment refactor is probably fine. This symptom is downstream: the selected model stream produced no non-empty visible chunks, yet the responder treated that as a successful empty response.
> 
> Two observations:
> 
> - `execution_runs: []` means this was **not** an `llm.smart`/JS-runtime failure. No ` ```plan` footer was parsed.
> - `"model": None` in `explain()` is a separate display bug: it reads the generic registered `PlanSpec`, whose `model_id` is intentionally `None`, rather than the materialized `ResponseRecord`.
> 
> ## 1. Fix `explain()` model display
> 
> In `planner2.py`, change:
> 
> ```python
> "model": plan.model_id,
> ```
> 
> to:
> 
> ```python
> "model": {
>     "id": record.model_id,
>     "version": record.model_version,
>     "resolution": dict(record.model_resolution),
> },
> ```
> 
> ## 2. Fail loudly on an empty provider stream
> 
> In `JavaScriptPlanningResponder.stream_response()`, find:
> 
> ```python
> initial_text, javascript_plan = splitter.finish()
> 
> # No plan: the initial response is final.
> if javascript_plan is None:
> ```
> 
> Insert this immediately after `splitter.finish()`:
> 
> ```python
> if not initial_raw:
>     raise RuntimeError(
>         "Model stream ended without emitting any chunks. "
>         f"model={plan.model_id!r}@{plan.model_version!r}"
>     )
> 
> if not initial_text.strip() and javascript_plan is None:
>     raise RuntimeError(
>         "Model stream produced no visible answer and no JavaScript plan. "
>         f"model={plan.model_id!r}@{plan.model_version!r}"
>     )
> ```
> 
> This will turn the misleading `completed` empty response into a diagnostic failure.
> 
